mihawk took sorrow in because of luffy  have you ever been so bored that you  went out of your way to start beef with  a yonko  well me neither however this guy has  mihawk is an incredibly interesting  character in the story for a number of  reasons as the strongest swordsman his  strength and position in the hierarchy  of the one piece world are probably one  of the most heatedly discussed topics in  the fandom at this point and while there  are probably just as many people who  find this debate pointless as those who  blow it way out of proportion  mihawk's power actually is a pretty big  part of his character because even  though he might not seem like the type  being one of the most stoic and serious  characters in the story mihawk has  actually a surprisingly interesting  character arc starting all the way back  at the baratier and coming to a close  with him taking zorro in as a  and student even though mihawk rarely  actively appears in the story he  actually plays a really important role  for one piece world building overall but  especially so for zorro and his journey  towards becoming the strongest swordsman  in the world so how did mihawk change as  a character during and after marine fort  how strong is he really what's the deal  with him and perona and will he ever  fight zoro and shanks again in the  future this is the story of the  strongest swordsman rival to shanks and  former warlord of the sea the story of  dracul mihawk  at his introduction early on in the  story mihawk serves as a signpost for  how strong people in the one piece world  truly are as a fellow warlord he  brilliantly sets the stakes for the  baroque work saga and the fight against  crocodile and i already talked a lot  about all the symbolism that is  associated with mihawk in my scene  analysis of his fight with zaro and  boratier so if you're interested in that  i suggest you check out that video  afterwards to sum it up though like most  swordsmen in the story mihawk has a very  strong religious theme going on giving  him the appearance of a spanish  inquisitor now this mostly serves to  show the duality of both his strong  dedication to his beliefs and creed  while at the same time highlighting his  violent unforgiving and strict side as  well just as the catholic church used to  be  during the inquisition  nobody expects the spanish inquisitor  now this creed that mihawk follows is  not christianity however but the bushido  the path of the samurai that zorro also  follows although approached through the  lens of buddhism while shanks represents  nordic mythology and its values and  beliefs long story short swordsmanship  and by that i mean real swordsmanship is  a sort of religion in the world of one  piece and only true believers and  followers can rise into the ranks of the  very best  and this serves as the framework for the  interactions and relationships that  mihawk has with both sorrow and shanks  all have quite different beliefs values  and powers but they all respect the  bushido and thus are worthy opponents  when it comes to sword fighting that is  and this is regardless of their actual  power level the reason why mihawk  suddenly started respecting zorro and  treated him as a worthy opponent is not  because zoro suddenly became as strong  as him but because he demonstrated his  will to follow the path of the swordsmen  all the way to death  and major spoiler alert from the manga  please skip ahead 10 seconds if you  aren't caught up yet  of course by dad will i also mean zaro's  congress hockey that mihawk most likely  saw when he used the 3 000 worlds  against him we actually have this really  short moment even in the manga as sora  launches his attack where mihawk is  clearly shocked by this reveal as it  serves as the ultimate proof of zoro's  will and potential to make it all the  way to the top so how is it that mihawk  went from seeing zoro as a potential  future rival to becoming his mentor  throughout the time skit for that let's  look at mihawk's surprisingly deep  character arc now writing a great  character arc is actually pretty damn  hard and as with all new skills you want  to learn it always helps having someone  explain it to you in great detail and  that is exactly what skillshare is all  about skillshare is an online learning  community with thousands of inspiring  classes for creative and curious people  covering pretty much any topic you might  ever want to learn about be it starting  a youtube channel or how you make your  very own manga i've personally been  using skillshare for quite some time now  mostly to improve my work here on  youtube but it can really be anything  for example i personally have really  enjoyed this course on knife skills by  elena carp recently so i can now slice  my onions in true mihawk fashion oh look  and there's even a class on  bladesmithing how fitting if you also  love learning new skills then you should  definitely give skillshare a try not  only is it pretty damn affordable with  under 10 bucks per month for an annual  membership but the first 1000 people to  use my link in the description below  will get a free trial of skillshare  premium so that you can try it out for  yourself but now back to my fellow knife  master  mihawk's perspective on the central  moral theme of one piece freedom is that  the best thing is to be a lone wolf  bound by no one and seeking out other  wolves to test his strength  or i guess in his case it's actually a  lone hawk one might even say he's a  mihawk  [Music]  now the major turning point for mihawk  was the war at marinefords before the  war we get introduced to him as an  extremely self-centered person he's  pretty much the only strong pirate out  there operating completely without any  crew or any other people supporting him  he is literally a one-man crew that  sails the seas bored out of his mind as  he has no one to test his ridiculous  strength on  this becomes especially clear in his  relationship with shanks regardless of  his ridiculous haki shanks was powerful  enough as a swordsman alone to spar  evenly with mihawk leading to the  legendary rivalry between the two no  doubt that mihawk came looking for  shanks quite often to have a duel but  when shang sacrifices his arm for luffy  and inspires him to become a pirate  mihawk doesn't care at all about his  motivations and the reasons he simply  sees shanks sacrificing his strong sword  arm as an insult with a rivalry and  doesn't even bother to draw his swords  against him  [Music]  shanks the only man who was ever able to  excite mihawk gave up on being a top  tier swordsman for a mere kit now given  that shanks has ridiculous hockey  abilities he probably doesn't care about  that though and their rivalry turns into  a sort of friendship  however while that was all nice and well  that now left mihawk with absolutely no  one to rival with and test his skills on  and he ended up so bored and aimless  that he chased after people like don  creek just to get a bit of a kick out of  it  [Music]  and i think it's pretty evident that  this self-centered person who only cared  about his own strength would never have  accepted zoro's show of throwing away  his pride as a swordsman by bowing to  him because this is essentially what  shanks did in mihawk's eyes as well and  so i think it comes as no big surprise  that this proud ridiculously powerful  swordsman despite becoming a warlord was  never really willing to follow the  directions of the world governments what  made the war at marine fort different  was not the fact that he was scared of  the world government's repercussions as  we just saw mihawk didn't really care at  all about losing his warlord title no  the reason he participated in this war  was because he would be able to  challenge white beards  the strongest man in the world versus  the strongest swordsman basically mihawk  went to marineford for a kick as well to  fight the only man he believed worth  fighting at this point and yet when he  showed his hand jozu shielded his  captain from his attack at this point it  begins to dawn on mihawk that whitebeard  wasn't only feared because of the beast  of a person he was  but also because of all the people he  had gathered around him and later the  very same thing happens again with luffy  deciding to test his resolve in power  again after already having taken a bit  of an interest in him at baratier more  and more of luffy's allies keep getting  in mihawk's way and i think it's at this  point that mihawk truly understands that  the ability of gathering strong allies  is the greatest power he's seen on the  seas  [Music]  and yet his mind doesn't change  completely here just yet when shanks  arrives to end the war he realizes that  there are no more worthy opponents left  to fight and he leaps shanks is the last  person he wants to fight after he ruined  the rivalry and of course to be fair  also because shanks as a yonko is most  likely still just out of his league when  he uses his hockey  and so things only really change from  mihawk after the war as zorro is  transported to his islands and it is  this interaction between them that  brings luffy's impact on mihawk to full  bloom zorro is the only character as far  as we know who mihawk hopes can grow to  one day challenge him and yet here zorro  is telling him that fighting for the  self-centered greatest swordsman pride  something that mihawk has only ever  cared about does no longer serve as his  motivation  wants to become the strongest swordsman  in order to help luffy become pirate  king and at first this clearly leaves  mihawk disgusted in a way he thinks that  zorro is weak and wasn't even strong  enough to beat all the monkeys on the  island that were trying to imitate him  by the way a really nice metaphor for  what he thinks of most other swordsmen  and he believed that zorro came asking  him for help to get off the island and  yet for the very first time these things  are now becoming more personal to mihawk  with added perspective when he sees that  zorro has easily beaten all the monkeys  and could leave whenever he wanted to  mihawk realizes that zorro is asking for  help for luffy's sake and not his own  which is why he agrees to train him  mihawk finally listens to zorro and  accepts his plea given that he's seen  that fighting for others is worthwhile  in its own right since luffy and  whitebeard fought for ace and in turn  many many people fought for them as well  ultimately making his presence at the  war more or less irrelevant especially  as he was never willing to go all out  anyways and so mihawk is now no longer  the lone wolf realizing that having  someone by your side to fight for is  what gives one true freedom and purpose  and so the fact that he lets perona and  zoro stay with him on this island for  two years forming real relationships for  the first time  is something that pre-war mihawk would  never have been able to do and  ultimately i believe that mihawk's  character arc would be fully completed  if he ever decided to spar with shanks  again
 

